---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('sequenzy')!;
const b = appBySlug('privy')!;
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-privy');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-privy');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Sequenzy or Privy for cart recovery?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Privy when onsite capture and follow-up need to work together. Evaluate Sequenzy when its Shopify email and SMS recovery fits the wider lifecycle work your team needs. Demonstrate the required journey before choosing."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a popup signup make every cart recoverable?",
    "answer": "No. Test whether the signup identity is associated with the basket and whether the intended message is permitted. An email address alone does not establish SMS permission or prove a cart event reached the sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both platforms run together?",
    "answer": "A split setup needs an explicit data handoff and one owner for each message. Do not assume that a purchase or unsubscribe in one system cancels messages queued elsewhere."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should discounts be compared?",
    "answer": "Subtract the discount from revenue while keeping product cost unchanged. Include shipping subsidies, fees and message costs before assessing contribution. Check whether signup and recovery offers can stack."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should results be judged?",
    "answer": "Compare eligible contacts and net contribution under consistent attribution rules. A randomized control can help estimate incremental orders when sample size supports it; platform-attributed revenue alone cannot establish causality."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Compare the route from an onsite signup to a recoverable basket, then decide who should own the reminder.</p>
    <div class="mt-10"><TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Onsite capture', tool: b.name, reason: 'Evaluate the popup, basket identity and follow-up as one journey.' }, { label: 'Lifecycle workflows', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate Shopify recovery alongside the other email and SMS journeys you need.' }]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Start with the contact you cannot currently reach</h2>
      <p>A store losing visitors before they enter checkout has a different problem from a store with identified checkout abandoners and competing reminders. For the first store, the decision is whether an onsite form creates a useful, permitted recovery opportunity. For the second, it is whether the sender can maintain accurate basket context and end recovery after purchase. Installing another automation tool does not by itself solve either problem.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.privy.com/cart-abandonment">Privy's cart recovery overview</a> describes onsite Cart Saver popups and email and text reminders with Shopify cart details. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/changelog">changelog</a> also describes Checkout Abandonment Flows and cart blocks. That is a broader role than a basic newsletter tool. The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy Shopify listing</a> describes email and SMS cart and checkout recovery alongside welcome, post-purchase and win-back flows. These published capabilities, checked November 24, 2026, support a workflow comparison rather than an email-only assumption or an automatic winner.</p>
      <h2>Proposed trial: a popup appears before checkout</h2>
      <p>Use designated test profiles and a representative mobile theme. This is a proposed acceptance test, not a merchant result. Add a product, trigger the intended Privy display, and inspect whether it obstructs the checkout button or repeats after dismissal. A form can collect addresses while making buying harder; record completed checkout behavior as well as signup rate.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Dismiss the form:</strong> continue shopping without submitting. Inspect which identity and basket events exist. Do not treat an anonymous basket as a deliverable contact or mistake the absence of a reminder for a delivery failure.</li>
        <li><strong>Submit email only:</strong> record the wording and purpose of the signup, then inspect which email journey would start. Leave the phone field empty. Verify that the contact has not become eligible for text reminders merely by submitting an email form.</li>
        <li><strong>Advance to checkout:</strong> enter the same address and inspect whether the earlier basket and checkout resolve to one customer journey. Check for two reminders competing for the same order.</li>
        <li><strong>Change the basket:</strong> remove the advertised item or change its variant. Inspect the waiting message's product block and return link. A signup confirmation and a cart reminder have different content requirements.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Run the equivalent identification-to-recovery demonstration in Sequenzy using its actual Shopify setup. If Privy remains the form provider and Sequenzy becomes the sender, demonstrate the handoff rather than assuming an integration: specify the profile identifier, permission source, offer version, basket association and retry behavior. A subscriber import is insufficient evidence that the latest basket can be recovered.</p>
      <h2>Do not let a signup offer silently become a second discount</h2>
      <p>A shopper who sees a signup benefit should not receive a conflicting recovery offer because two systems own different parts of the journey. Record which code the form promises, its eligibility and expiry, and what happens when another code is already applied. Test both a valid redemption and an ineligible basket. Explain the actual restriction instead of adding artificial urgency.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical offer budget: a $513 basket at 52% product gross margin has $246.24 product cost and $266.76 product gross profit. An 8% discount removes $41.04 from revenue. At unchanged product cost, discounted revenue is $471.96 and product gross profit is $225.72. The profit reduction is the full $41.04 discount. Shipping, payment fees, returns and sending costs reduce contribution further. This is a calculation, not a forecast of recovered revenue.</p>
      <h2>Choose a message owner before enabling reminders</h2>
      <p>List the native Shopify, Privy and Sequenzy journeys that can reach this shopper. Assign an owner for each entry event and record the pending queue before a cutover. Complete a test purchase while a reminder waits, then inspect the actual eligibility decision in every active sender. Repeat with an unsubscribe and a replayed event. The intended outcome is a stopped ineligible message and no duplicate enrollment from a retry; an app connection banner cannot demonstrate either.</p>
      <p>Keep email and SMS permission records separate, including source and withdrawal state. Decide cadence from the buying cycle, customer response and permitted sending windows. Adding a text because an email was not opened is a proposal to evaluate, not proof of permission or a universal escalation rule. Avoid enabling both tools' default journeys while deciding which one to keep.</p>
      <h2>Compare the cost of the complete capture-to-order journey</h2>
      <p>Request quotes against the same reachable audience, email volume, SMS destinations and expected segments. Include form costs, paid flow features, overages and the work required to maintain any handoff. Compare a single-platform configuration with the actual split configuration you would operate. A low starting subscription does not establish the cheaper implementation.</p>
      <p>Measure eligible visitors, submitted forms, identified baskets, reminders and completed orders as separate stages. A popup may grow the subscriber list without increasing profitable orders. Compare net contribution with consistent attribution windows; if traffic supports a randomized control, size it for the decision rather than assigning an arbitrary percentage. Neither vendor's recovery dashboard alone establishes which purchases the reminder caused.</p>
      <h2>What would justify choosing each?</h2>
      <p>Privy merits evaluation when onsite capture is central to the recovery plan and the same team wants to manage the display and subsequent messages. Sequenzy merits evaluation when Shopify recovery needs to fit a broader lifecycle program and the required flows can be maintained through its available tools. A split setup is useful only if the capture benefit justifies the data handoff and operating work. Choose from the demonstrated journey, maintainability and matched cost, without treating a feature label as a revenue result.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs Privy FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
